How does a human log in, and keep the session alive?

Two commands start a link and supply an OIDC credential. Use `auth-agent`
for an interactive session and `connect` for scripts.

  Command                    Starts    Reports the    Stays resident
                             the link  outcome
  -------------------------  --------  -------------  --------------------
  ph-cli auth-agent <id>     yes       no             yes - until
                                                      interrupted
  ph-cli connect <id>        yes       yes, with      no - returns as soon
                                       exit codes     as the link is up

A resident `auth-agent` renews your authentication silently (zipline#66),
so you log in once per session ceiling (`max_auth_age_seconds`) instead of
once per `expiration_seconds`. The refresh token is held in this process's
memory; when a renewal comes due, the packet handler asks the agent for a
fresh credential and it is served non-interactively, with no browser. Run
`auth-agent` and leave it resident.

So the interactive flow is TWO steps, not three:

  1. ph (the adapter) running          - daemon, systemd
  2. ph-cli auth-agent <id>            - starts the link, the browser opens
                                         once, the process stays resident

`ph-cli connect` is redundant there. It keeps its value for CI and scripts,
where the blocking outcome report and the exit-code contract (2 declined,
3 timeout, 4 IdP unreachable, 5 visa service rejected the token, 6 policy
denied, 7 device blob rejected) are the point.

While ph needs root for the tun interface and the control socket is not yet
reachable unprivileged for every deployment (zipline#39), the documented
form is:

  sudo ph-cli auth-agent 2 --no-browser

`--no-browser` is the norm under sudo, not a workaround: a root process
cannot usefully open the user's browser, and `xdg-open` fails silently after
a successful spawn, so the printed URL is pasted into the user's own browser
instead. That browser then redirects to http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback -
a plain loopback connect into the ph-cli process - so the cross-user
boundary does not matter. The paste is bounded by a 300 s login timeout.
The paste is needed once per session ceiling
(`max_auth_age_seconds`) rather than once per `expiration_seconds`.

Do NOT run `connect` after `auth-agent` on the same link. Both register as
the link's authentication agent and the second registration OVERWRITES the
first, so a `connect` run "just to check" replaces a live resident agent
with one that is about to exit -- silently, with nothing logged. Use
`ph-cli link show <id>` to check on a link instead; it reports the
authentication expiry and the last authentication failure.

Which socket does ph-cli talk to?

ph-cli needs no sudo. By default it searches for the packet handler's
control socket in this order (zipline#39):

1. The per-user socket for your uid: /var/run/zpr/<uid>/control.sock.
   This is where a sudo- or pkexec-started ph puts it, owned by the user
   who ran sudo, mode 0600. The base /var/run/zpr is fixed (never derived
   from HOME/XDG_DATA_HOME) so ph and ph-cli agree across the sudo
   privilege boundary.
2. The shared socket: <data_home>/zpr/control.sock. This is where a
   systemd-started ph puts it; if the host has a "zpr" group the socket is
   group zpr, mode 0660, so group members can use ph-cli directly.

If neither answers, ph-cli fails with an error naming both paths it tried.
A candidate is selected by probe-connecting to it, not by checking the
pathname exists, so a stale socket file left by a dead ph never shadows a
live server at the other path.
An explicit -p (control socket) or -c (capture socket) always wins and
short-circuits the search. When the control socket was found by the default
search, the default capture socket is capture.sock in the same directory —
the pair belongs to the same adapter by construction. With an explicit -p
and no -c, the derived capture candidates (per-uid, then shared) are probed
first, because a ph started with only --control-path keeps its capture
socket at the derived default; capture.sock beside the explicit control
path is the last-resort fallback.

Security note: reaching the control socket means being able to start and
stop links and register an AuthAgent — i.e. to supply and observe user
credentials for this adapter, and to receive the IdP client_secret in a
HelloResponse. Socket access is adapter control; the zpr group is not a
low-privilege convenience.

How to generate Bash completion script 

Compile the program using cargo build

Run the program using the generate flag and the path to the file:

./target/debug/ph-cli --generate <FILE_PATH>

Run 'source ./FILE_PATH' 
This will only work for your session,if you want it to be permanent, you can 
add it to /etc/bash_completion.d or put source ./ph-cli.bash in your 
.bashrc or .bash_profile

Now run the program:

target/debug/ph-cli

And you can tab complete command line arguments 
